\date{2025-11-29T18:12:16Z}
\title{Closure}
\taxon{Definition}
\tag{discrete}

\p{A collection of numbers (or other items) is \em{closed} under a
certain operation if doing that operation on two of the numbers always
gives another such number.  For example, the [natural numbers](002D) are
closed under addition (that is, adding any two natural numbers gives
another natural number) and under multiplication (multipltying any two
natural numbers gives another natural number).
\pre\verb<<<|
Disco> :type 3 + 5
3 + 5 : ℕ
Disco> :type 3 * 5
3 * 5 : ℕ<<<
However, the natural numbers are \em{not} closed under
subtraction, because subtracting two natural numbers might not always
result in another natural number.  For example, #{2 - 5} is not a natural
number.}